        "title": "Harmonic Functions and the Dirichlet Problem",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Harmonic functions are solutions to Laplace's equation ∇²u = 0. They appear in electrostatics (electric potential), steady-state heat conduction, fluid potential flow, and gravitational theory. Complex analysis provides the most powerful two-dimensional toolkit for harmonic functions: every harmonic function is locally the real part of a holomorphic function, and conformal maps preserve harmonicity. The Dirichlet problem (find a harmonic function with prescribed boundary values) is solved explicitly on the disk and upper half-plane via the Poisson formula."
    },
